  "final_report": {
    "Incident": "Bumblebee Malware Returns After Recent Law Enforcement Disruption",
    "Root cause": "Phishing emails lure victims to download malicious ZIP archives containing a .LNK shortcut that triggers PowerShell to download a malicious MSI file from a remote server. *New phishing campaigns also pretend to be voicemail notifications, using OneDrive URLs to download Word documents with malicious macros. The malware now uses MSI files disguised as legitimate software and executes malicious code directly in memory, avoiding disk writes. Additionally, a new variant called Lumma Stealer has emerged, employing fake CAPTCHA pages to deceive users* (https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/bumblebee-malware-attacks-are-back-after-4-month-break/; https://cybersecsentinel.com/bumblebee-malware-adapts-to-evade-detection-in-new-attack-campaign/; https://securityaffairs.com/170306/malware/security-affairs-malware-newsletter-round-17.html). *Google’s Threat Analysis Group (TAG) first discovered the malware in March 2022 and named it Bumblebee based on a user-agent string it used. In the new infection chain, analyzed samples are disguised as Nvidia and Midjourney installers, loading and executing the final payload entirely in memory* (https://www.infosecurity-magazine.com/news/possible-bumblebee-resurgence/).",
    "Threat Actor/group/campaign": "The Bumblebee malware is believed to be the creation of TrickBot developers, used by ransomware threat actors. *Additionally, it is replacing the BazarLoader backdoor in cyberattacks by the Conti syndicate. Likely Trickbot or Conti affiliates are behind the new wave of attacks. Reports indicate that threat actors are utilizing advanced social engineering techniques similar to those seen in recent Black Basta campaigns. The malware has been linked to several ransomware groups, including Conti, Quantum, and MountLocker, all of which use it as part of their initial access strategy* (https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/new-bumblebee-malware-replaces-contis-bazarloader-in-cyberattacks/; https://cybersecsentinel.com/bumblebee-malware-adapts-to-evade-detection-in-new-attack-campaign/; https://securityaffairs.com/170306/malware/security-affairs-malware-newsletter-round-17.html; https://www.infosecurity-magazine.com/news/possible-bumblebee-resurgence/).",
    "Organization/industry/location": "The specific targets are not mentioned, but the malware is generally aimed at providing ransomware threat actors access to victim networks. *Recent campaigns have targeted thousands of organizations in the United States and emphasized critical infrastructure, with some attacks leveraging vulnerabilities such as the SonicWall VPN flaw CVE-2024-40766* (https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/bumblebee-malware-attacks-are-back-after-4-month-break/; https://cybersecsentinel.com/bumblebee-malware-adapts-to-evade-detection-in-new-attack-campaign/; https://securityaffairs.com/170306/malware/security-affairs-malware-newsletter-round-17.html).",
    "Start date – End date": "The recent resurgence was observed more than four months after Europol's 'Operation Endgame' in May 2024. *The return of Bumblebee was observed since October 2023, and the latest threat surfaced in October 2024* (https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/bumblebee-malware-attacks-are-back-after-4-month-break/; https://cybersecsentinel.com/bumblebee-malware-adapts-to-evade-detection-in-new-attack-campaign/; https://www.infosecurity-magazine.com/news/possible-bumblebee-resurgence/).",
    "MITRE TTPs": [
      "T1566.001: Phishing",
      "T1195.002: Malvertising",
      "T1071.001: Application Layer Protocol",
      "T1059.001: PowerShell",
      "T1055: Process Injection",
      "T1204.002: User Execution: Malicious File"
    ],
    "Impact": "The exact scale of the campaign is not provided, but Bumblebee is known to deliver Cobalt Strike beacons, information-stealing malware, and various ransomware strains. *Bumblebee also integrates sophisticated evasion techniques and anti-analysis tricks, executing malware entirely in memory through the SelfReg table within MSI files, and has a high threat score of 8.5/10* (https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/new-bumblebee-malware-replaces-contis-bazarloader-in-cyberattacks/; https://cybersecsentinel.com/bumblebee-malware-adapts-to-evade-detection-in-new-attack-campaign/; https://www.infosecurity-magazine.com/news/possible-bumblebee-resurgence/).",
