  "final_report": {
    "Incident": "Bumblebee Malware Returns After Recent Law Enforcement Disruption",
    "Root cause": "Phishing emails lure victims to download malicious ZIP archives containing a .LNK shortcut that triggers PowerShell to download a malicious MSI file from a remote server. *New phishing campaigns also pretend to be voicemail notifications, using OneDrive URLs to download Word documents with malicious macros. The malware now uses MSI files disguised as legitimate software and executes malicious code directly in memory, avoiding disk writes. Additionally, a new variant called Lumma Stealer has emerged, employing fake CAPTCHA pages to deceive users* (https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/bumblebee-malware-attacks-are-back-after-4-month-break/; https://cybersecsentinel.com/bumblebee-malware-adapts-to-evade-detection-in-new-attack-campaign/; https://securityaffairs.com/170306/malware/security-affairs-malware-newsletter-round-17.html). *Google’s Threat Analysis Group (TAG) first discovered the malware in March 2022 and named it Bumblebee based on a user-agent string it used. In the new infection chain, analyzed samples are disguised as Nvidia and Midjourney installers, loading and executing the final payload entirely in memory. The malware uses ISO files delivered through compromised email chains and Google Cloud links or password protected zip folders. The ISO files contain hidden DLLs and LNK files that initiate the infection process* (https://www.infosecurity-magazine.com/news/possible-bumblebee-resurgence/; https://cloudsek.com/blog/technical-analysis-of-bumblebee-malware-loader).",
