# Enriched Doc (enrihcments marked with *content*(link)): 
 Incident: Bumblebee Malware Returns After Recent Law Enforcement Disruption 

 Root cause: Phishing emails lure victims to download malicious ZIP archives containing a .LNK shortcut that triggers PowerShell to download a malicious MSI file from a remote server. *New phishing campaigns also pretend to be voicemail notifications, using OneDrive URLs to download Word documents with malicious macros. The malware now uses MSI files disguised as legitimate software and executes malicious code directly in memory, avoiding disk writes. Additionally, a new variant called Lumma Stealer has emerged, employing fake CAPTCHA pages to deceive users* (https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/bumblebee-malware-attacks-are-back-after-4-month-break/; https://cybersecsentinel.com/bumblebee-malware-adapts-to-evade-detection-in-new-attack-campaign/; https://securityaffairs.com/170306/malware/security-affairs-malware-newsletter-round-17.html). *Google’s Threat Analysis Group (TAG) first discovered the malware in March 2022 and named it Bumblebee based on a user-agent string it used. In the new infection chain, analyzed samples are disguised as Nvidia and Midjourney installers, loading and executing the final payload entirely in memory. The malware uses ISO files delivered through compromised email chains and Google Cloud links or password protected zip folders. The ISO files contain hidden DLLs and LNK files that initiate the infection process* (https://www.infosecurity-magazine.com/news/possible-bumblebee-resurgence/; https://cloudsek.com/blog/technical-analysis-of-bumblebee-malware-loader). 

 Threat Actor/group/campaign: The Bumblebee malware is believed to be the creation of TrickBot developers, used by ransomware threat actors. *Additionally, it is replacing the BazarLoader backdoor in cyberattacks by the Conti syndicate. Likely Trickbot or Conti affiliates are behind the new wave of attacks. Reports indicate that threat actors are utilizing advanced social engineering techniques similar to those seen in recent Black Basta campaigns. The malware has been linked to several ransomware groups, including Conti, Quantum, and MountLocker, all of which use it as part of their initial access strategy. Threat group TA578 has also been using Bumblebee in their campaigns* (https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/new-bumblebee-malware-replaces-contis-bazarloader-in-cyberattacks/; https://cybersecsentinel.com/bumblebee-malware-adapts-to-evade-detection-in-new-attack-campaign/; https://securityaffairs.com/170306/malware/security-affairs-malware-newsletter-round-17.html; https://www.infosecurity-magazine.com/news/possible-bumblebee-resurgence/; https://cloudsek.com/blog/technical-analysis-of-bumblebee-malware-loader). 

 Organization/industry/location: The specific targets are not mentioned, but the malware is generally aimed at providing ransomware threat actors access to victim networks. *Recent campaigns have targeted thousands of organizations in the United States and emphasized critical infrastructure, with some attacks leveraging vulnerabilities such as the SonicWall VPN flaw CVE-2024-40766* (https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/bumblebee-malware-attacks-are-back-after-4-month-break/; https://cybersecsentinel.com/bumblebee-malware-adapts-to-evade-detection-in-new-attack-campaign/; https://securityaffairs.com/170306/malware/security-affairs-malware-newsletter-round-17.html). 

 Start date – End date: The recent resurgence was observed more than four months after Europol's 'Operation Endgame' in May 2024. *The return of Bumblebee was observed since October 2023, and the latest threat surfaced in October 2024* (https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/bumblebee-malware-attacks-are-back-after-4-month-break/; https://cybersecsentinel.com/bumblebee-malware-adapts-to-evade-detection-in-new-attack-campaign/; https://www.infosecurity-magazine.com/news/possible-bumblebee-resurgence/). 

 MITRE TTPs: ['T1566.001: Phishing', 'T1195.002: Malvertising', 'T1071.001: Application Layer Protocol', 'T1059.001: PowerShell', 'T1055: Process Injection', 'T1204.002: User Execution: Malicious File'] 

 Impact: The exact scale of the campaign is not provided, but Bumblebee is known to deliver Cobalt Strike beacons, information-stealing malware, and various ransomware strains. *Bumblebee also integrates sophisticated evasion techniques and anti-analysis tricks, executing malware entirely in memory through the SelfReg table within MSI files, and has a high threat score of 8.5/10. The malware is capable of performing token manipulation, code injections, and employs anti-VM checks* (https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/new-bumblebee-malware-replaces-contis-bazarloader-in-cyberattacks/; https://cybersecsentinel.com/bumblebee-malware-adapts-to-evade-detection-in-new-attack-campaign/; https://www.infosecurity-magazine.com/news/possible-bumblebee-resurgence/; https://cloudsek.com/blog/technical-analysis-of-bumblebee-malware-loader). 

 Mitigation: {'Email Security': ['Implement advanced email filtering to block phishing emails.', 'Educate employees about phishing scams and how to recognize them.', '*Reinforce phishing awareness training to avoid interaction with suspicious emails and attachments* (https://cybersecsentinel.com/bumblebee-malware-adapts-to-evade-detection-in-new-attack-campaign/).'], 'Endpoint Protection': ['Use endpoint protection solutions that can detect and block malicious files.', 'Regularly update antivirus and anti-malware software.', '*Ensure endpoint security solutions can detect and mitigate in-memory malware operations* (https://cybersecsentinel.com/bumblebee-malware-adapts-to-evade-detection-in-new-attack-campaign/).'], 'Network Security': ['Monitor network traffic for unusual activities, such as unexpected downloads of MSI files.', 'Implement network segmentation to limit the spread of malware.'], 'Patching and Updates': ['Ensure all software is up-to-date with the latest security patches.', '*Regularly review logs for suspicious activities, especially fileless or in-memory operations* (https://cybersecsentinel.com/bumblebee-malware-adapts-to-evade-detection-in-new-attack-campaign/).'], 'Backup and Recovery': ['Regularly back up important data and ensure backups are not connected to main networks.'], 'Incident Response': ['Develop and regularly update an incident response plan.']} 

 Detection Signature: {'Service': 'PowerShell', 'Port': 'N/A', 'Severity': 'High', 'Incident': 'Bumblebee Malware Resurgence', 'Signature name': 'Bumblebee MSI File Download', 'Internal checks': {'Setting1': 'Monitor for the execution of PowerShell scripts that download files from the internet.', 'Setting2': 'Detect the use of msiexec.exe with suspicious parameters (e.g., /qn for silent installation).', 'Setting3': 'Monitor for the creation of .LNK files that execute PowerShell.'}, 'External scanning': ['Monitor for phishing emails containing ZIP files or .LNK shortcuts.', 'Detect outgoing traffic to known malicious domains or IP addresses used by Bumblebee.']} 

