a> </li> <li> <a href="/blog/category/resources"> <span class="underline"> Resources </span> </a> </li> <li> <a href="/blog/category/videos"> <span class="underline"> Videos </span> </a> </li> <li> <a href="/blog/category/news"> <span class="underline"> News </span> </a> </li> </ul> </div> </div> </div> <!-- MOBILE Search and Subscribe --> <div class="container-is-blog columns is-gapless is-hidden-desktop cr-mln__search-subscribe--mobile"> <div class="column"> <a class="search-btn"> Search </a> </div> <div class="column"> <a class="#" href="#blog-subscribe"> Subscribe </a> </div> </div> <!-- END MOBILE Search and Subscribe --> <!-- SEARCH Modal Wrap --> <div class="animated cr-search-modal-off" id="cr-search-modal" style="position: fixed; width: 100%; height: 100%; top: 0px; left: 0px; background-color: rgb(235, 235, 235); overflow-y: auto; z-index: -9999; opacity: 0; animation-duration: 0.6s;"> <!--THIS IS IMPORTANT! to close the modal, the class name has to match the name given on the ID --> <div class="close-cr-search-modal" id="btn-close-modal"> X </div> <div class="modal-content"> <div class="container columns"> <div class="column"> <div class="cr-search-modal__search-bar"> <h3> Search </h3> <form action="/hs-search-results"> <input autocomplete="on" class="hs-search-field__input" name="term" placeholder="Search..." type="search"/> <input name="type" type="hidden" value="BLOG_POST"/> <input name="type" type="hidden" value="LISTING_PAGE"/> <button class="arrow" type="submit"> </button> </form> </div> </div> </div> </div> </div> <!-- END Search Modal Wrap --> </section> </div> </div> <!--end widget-span --> </div> <!--end row--> </div> <!--end row-wrapper --> </div> <!--end header --> </div> <!--end header wrapper --> <div class="body-container-wrapper"> <div class="body-container container-fluid"> <div class="row-fluid-wrapper row-depth-1 row-number-1"> <div class="row-fluid"> <div class="span12 widget-span widget-type-blog_content" data-w="12" data-widget-type="blog_content" data-x="0" style=""> <div class="cr-mln__blog-post"> <div class="container-is-blog columns is-multiline page-center"> <div class="column is-8-fullhd is-8-desktop is-offset-2-fullhd is-offset-2-desktop is-10-tablet is-offset-1-tablet"> <div class="featured-image"> <img alt="" src="https://www.cybereason.com/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/featured-images/beast-ransomware-blog-featured.png"/> </div> <h1> <span class="hs_cos_wrapper hs_cos_wrapper_meta_field hs_cos_wrapper_type_text" data-hs-cos-general-type="meta_field" data-hs-cos-type="text" id="hs_cos_wrapper_name" style=""> THREAT ANALYSIS: Beast Ransomware </span> </h1> <div class="cr-mln__post-author-share"> <div class="hubspot-editable cr-mln__post-meta" data-hubspot-form-id="author_data" data-hubspot-name="Blog Author" id="hubspot-author_data"> <span class="descriptor"> Written By </span> <p> <span class="author"> Cybereason Security Services Team </span> </p> </div> </div> </div> <!-- Sticky Author and Social Box --> <!-- END Sticky Author and Social Box --> <div class="container-is-blog columns is-multiline page-center cr-mln__blog-post--body"> <div class="column is-7-fullhd is-7-desktop is-10-tablet is-10-mobile is-offset-1-fullhd is-offset-1-desktop is-offset-1-tablet is-offset-1-mobile"> <span class="hs_cos_wrapper hs_cos_wrapper_meta_field hs_cos_wrapper_type_rich_text" data-hs-cos-general-type="meta_field" data-hs-cos-type="rich_text" id="hs_cos_wrapper_post_body" style=""> <p> Cybereason issues Threat Analysis reports to investigate emerging threats and provide practical recommendations for protecting against them. In this Threat Analysis report, Cybereason investigates the Ransomware-as-a-Service (RaaS) known as Beast and how to defend against it through the <a href="https://www.cybereason.com/platform#graphic"> <span> Cybereason Defense Platform </span> </a> . </p> <!--more--> <h3 style="font-size: 30px; font-weight: normal;"> <span style="color: #000000;"> KEY POINTS </span> </h3> <ul> <li aria-level="1"> <strong> Expanding Marketplace: </strong> The Beast Ransomware group provides various tools with constant version updates. These updates are made to appeal to wider audiences across the underground cybercrime ecosystem. </li> </ul> <ul> <li aria-level="1"> <strong> Binary Customizations: </strong> The Beast RaaS platform offers affiliates numerous options for building ransomware binaries that target Windows, Linux, and ESXi systems, enabling tailored configurations to suit different operational requirements. </li> <li aria-level="1"> <strong> Detection And Prevention: </strong> The Cybereason Defense Platform employs advanced Anti-Ransomware and Anti-Malware features, designed to detect and block ransomware payloads like Beast before they can execute. <br/> <br/> </li> </ul> <h3 style="font-size: 30px; font-weight: normal;"> <span style="color: #000000;"> INTRODUCTION </span> </h3> <p> The Beast Ransomware group has been active since 2022. Recently, a Beast Ransomware partnership program and new capabilities were promoted on the underground forums in June. The group has updated and created various versions to meet the market demand. </p> <p> <img alt="beast-ransomware-blog-1" height="217" loading="lazy" sizes="(max-width: 886px) 100vw, 886px" src="https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-1.png?width=886&amp;height=217&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-1.png" srcset="https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-1.png?width=443&amp;height=109&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-1.png 443w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-1.png?width=886&amp;height=217&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-1.png 886w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-1.png?width=1329&amp;height=326&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-1.png 1329w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-1.png?width=1772&amp;height=434&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-1.png 1772w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-1.png?width=2215&amp;height=543&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-1.png 2215w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-1.png?width=2658&amp;height=651&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-1.png 2658w" style="height: auto; max-width: 100%; width: 886px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block;" width="886"/> </p> <p style="text-align: center; font-size: 16px;"> <em> Invitation to cooperate in Russian, English and Chinese languages. </em> </p> <p> Previous versions of the Beast Ransomware, also known as Monster, were developed using the Delphi programming language and offered as a Ransomware-as-a-Service (RaaS) platform. </p> <p> <img alt="beast-ransomware-blog-2" height="363" loading="lazy" sizes="(max-width: 870px) 100vw, 870px" src="https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-2.jpg?width=870&amp;height=363&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-2.jpg" srcset="https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-2.jpg?width=435&amp;height=182&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-2.jpg 435w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-2.jpg?width=870&amp;height=363&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-2.jpg 870w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-2.jpg?width=1305&amp;height=545&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-2.jpg 1305w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-2.jpg?width=1740&amp;height=726&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-2.jpg 1740w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-2.jpg?width=2175&amp;height=908&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-2.jpg 2175w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-2.jpg?width=2610&amp;height=1089&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-2.jpg 2610w" style="height: auto; max-width: 100%; width: 870px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block;" width="870"/> </p> <p style="text-align: center;"> <span style="font-size: 16px;"> <em> First Appearance Of Beast Ransomware On The Russian Anonymous Marketplace </em> </span> </p> <h3 style="font-size: 30px; font-weight: normal;"> <span style="color: #000000;"> TECHNICAL ANALYSIS </span> </h3> <h3 style="font-size: 30px; font-weight: normal;"> <span style="color: #000000;"> Beast Operating System Support – Windows </span> </h3> <p> The current known Windows versions of Beast demonstrate the following capabilities: </p> <ul> <li aria-level="1"> Combination of Elliptic-curve and ChaCha20 encryption model </li> <li aria-level="1"> Written in the C programming language </li> </ul> <p style="padding-left: 40px;"> <img alt="beast-ransomware-blog-3" height="231" loading="lazy" sizes="(max-width: 570px) 100vw, 570px" src="https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-3.png?width=570&amp;height=231&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-3.png" srcset="https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-3.png?width=285&amp;height=116&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-3.png 285w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-3.png?width=570&amp;height=231&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-3.png 570w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-3.png?width=855&amp;height=347&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-3.png 855w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-3.png?width=1140&amp;height=462&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-3.png 1140w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-3.png?width=1425&amp;height=578&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-3.png 1425w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-3.png?width=1710&amp;height=693&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-3.png 1710w" style="height: auto; max-width: 100%; width: 570px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block;" width="570"/> </p> <p style="text-align: center; padding-left: 40px; font-size: 16px;"> <em> Beast Windows Binary </em> </p> <ul> <li aria-level="1"> Segmented file encryption </li> <li aria-level="1"> ZIP wrapper mode - Files are converted on the fly to <em> .zip </em> with ransom note inside </li> <li aria-level="1"> Multithreaded queue for encryption </li> <li aria-level="1"> Processes/Services termination </li> <li aria-level="1"> Shadow copy delete </li> <li aria-level="1"> Mounting hidden partitions </li> <li aria-level="1"> Subnet scanner </li> <li aria-level="1"> In August 2024, offline builder was promoted with option to configure builds for Windows, NAS, ESXi. <br/> <br/> </li> </ul> <p> <img alt="beast-ransomware-blog-4" height="564" loading="lazy" sizes="(max-width: 875px) 100vw, 875px" src="https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-4.jpg?width=875&amp;height=564&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-4.jpg" srcset="https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-4.jpg?width=438&amp;height=282&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-4.jpg 438w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-4.jpg?width=875&amp;height=564&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-4.jpg 875w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-4.jpg?width=1313&amp;height=846&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-4.jpg 1313w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-4.jpg?width=1750&amp;height=1128&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-4.jpg 1750w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-4.jpg?width=2188&amp;height=1410&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-4.jpg 2188w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-4.jpg?width=2625&amp;height=1692&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-4.jpg 2625w" style="height: auto; max-width: 100%; width: 875px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block;" width="875"/> </p> <p style="text-align: center; font-size: 16px;"> <em> New Beast Offline Builder </em> </p> <h3 style="font-size: 30px; font-weight: normal;"> <span style="color: #000000;"> Beast Operating System Support – Linux And ESXi </span> </h3> <p> The Beast Linux version has the following capabilities (controllable via command line argument): </p> <ul> <li aria-level="1"> Selectable path for encryption </li> <li aria-level="1"> Enable/disable certain functionality </li> <li aria-level="1"> Ransom note generation from external file </li> <li aria-level="1"> Daemon mode </li> <li aria-level="1"> Written in C and Go programming languages </li> </ul> <p> The VMWare ESXi version also has the following additional options: </p> <ul> <li aria-level="1"> Option to shut down a VM and machine’s files encryption </li> <li aria-level="1"> Option to exclude some <em> vmid </em> </li> </ul> <p> </p> <p> <img alt="beast-ransomware-blog-5" height="557" loading="lazy" sizes="(max-width: 1003px) 100vw, 1003px" src="https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-5.jpg?width=1003&amp;height=557&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-5.jpg" srcset="https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-5.jpg?width=502&amp;height=279&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-5.jpg 502w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-5.jpg?width=1003&amp;height=557&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-5.jpg 1003w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-5.jpg?width=1505&amp;height=836&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-5.jpg 1505w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-5.jpg?width=2006&amp;height=1114&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-5.jpg 2006w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-5.jpg?width=2508&amp;height=1393&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-5.jpg 2508w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-5.jpg?width=3009&amp;height=1671&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-5.jpg 3009w" style="height: auto; max-width: 100%; width: 1003px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block;" width="1003"/> </p> <p style="text-align: center; font-size: 16px;"> <em> Linux &amp; ESXi Version Parameters </em> </p> <h3 style="font-size: 30px; font-weight: normal;"> <span style="color: #000000;"> Binary Analysis - BEAST HERE? </span> </h3> <p> Like most ransomware, the initial compromise often occurs through various infection vectors, such as phishing emails, or compromised remote desktop protocol (RDP) endpoints. </p> <p> To prevent multiple instances of Beast running simultaneously on the same system, it creates a unique mutex with the string “BEAST HERE?”. This ensures efficient execution and enables the attacker to maintain control over the ransomware’s behavior on the infected system. </p> <p style="text-align: center;"> <span style="font-size: 14px;"> <img alt="beast-ransomware-blog-6" height="133" loading="lazy" sizes="(max-width: 667px) 100vw, 667px" src="https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-6.png?width=667&amp;height=133&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-6.png" srcset="https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-6.png?width=334&amp;height=67&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-6.png 334w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-6.png?width=667&amp;height=133&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-6.png 667w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-6.png?width=1001&amp;height=200&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-6.png 1001w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-6.png?width=1334&amp;height=266&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-6.png 1334w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-6.png?width=1668&amp;height=333&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-6.png 1668w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-6.png?width=2001&amp;height=399&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-6.png 2001w" style="height: auto; max-width: 100%; width: 667px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block;" width="667"/> </span> </p> <p style="text-align: center;"> <span style="font-size: 16px;"> <em> Beast Creates A Mutex Object With BEAST HERE? String </em> </span> </p> <p> The latest version of Beast Ransomware specifically avoids encrypting data on devices located in Commonwealth of Independent States (CIS) countries, such as Russia, Belarus, and Moldova. This is achieved through code that checks the system's default language settings, country code, and retrieves the target's IP address. </p> <p> If the ransomware detects that the device is in a CIS country, it halts encryption activities. This strategic exclusion is likely a tactic to avoid drawing attention or repercussions from authorities in those regions. </p> <p> <img alt="beast-ransomware-blog-7" height="43" loading="lazy" sizes="(max-width: 925px) 100vw, 925px" src="https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-7.png?width=925&amp;height=43&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-7.png" srcset="https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-7.png?width=463&amp;height=22&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-7.png 463w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-7.png?width=925&amp;height=43&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-7.png 925w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-7.png?width=1388&amp;height=65&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-7.png 1388w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-7.png?width=1850&amp;height=86&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-7.png 1850w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-7.png?width=2313&amp;height=108&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-7.png 2313w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-7.png?width=2775&amp;height=129&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-7.png 2775w" style="height: auto; max-width: 100%; width: 925px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block;" width="925"/> </p> <p style="text-align: center;"> <span style="font-size: 16px;"> <em> Checking Victim IP &amp; Location By Connecting To iplogger.co </em> </span> </p> <p> Beast performs SMB scans to automatically search for and infect vulnerable computers on nearby networks. This self-propagation mechanism can quickly spread the payload without requiring any human intervention. </p> <p> <img alt="beast-ransomware-blog-8" height="339" loading="lazy" sizes="(max-width: 633px) 100vw, 633px" src="https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-8.png?width=633&amp;height=339&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-8.png" srcset="https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-8.png?width=317&amp;height=170&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-8.png 317w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-8.png?width=633&amp;height=339&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-8.png 633w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-8.png?width=950&amp;height=509&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-8.png 950w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-8.png?width=1266&amp;height=678&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-8.png 1266w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-8.png?width=1583&amp;height=848&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-8.png 1583w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-8.png?width=1899&amp;height=1017&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-8.png 1899w" style="height: auto; max-width: 100%; width: 633px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block;" width="633"/> </p> <p style="text-align: center; font-size: 16px;"> Beast SMB Scanning </p> <h3 style="font-size: 30px; font-weight: normal;"> <span style="color: #434343;"> Load Of RstrtMgr DLL (Restart Manager) </span> </h3> <p> <em> RstrtMgr.dll </em> , the Restart Manager, is a critical system component that safeguards open and unsaved files during system reboots. It acts as a gatekeeper, prompting users to save their work before shutting down to prevent data loss. Beast Ransomware exploits this DLL in a malicious way. </p> <p> Before encrypting a file, the ransomware stops services and processes in order to unlock and safely close open files. </p> <p style="text-align: center;"> <img alt="beast-ransomware-blog-9" height="48" loading="lazy" sizes="(max-width: 342px) 100vw, 342px" src="https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-9.png?width=342&amp;height=48&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-9.png" srcset="https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-9.png?width=171&amp;height=24&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-9.png 171w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-9.png?width=342&amp;height=48&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-9.png 342w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-9.png?width=513&amp;height=72&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-9.png 513w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-9.png?width=684&amp;height=96&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-9.png 684w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-9.png?width=855&amp;height=120&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-9.png 855w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-9.png?width=1026&amp;height=144&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-9.png 1026w" style="height: auto; max-width: 100%; width: 342px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block;" width="342"/> </p> <p> <img alt="beast-ransomware-blog-10" height="141" loading="lazy" sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 700px" src="https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-10.png?width=700&amp;height=141&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-10.png" srcset="https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-10.png?width=350&amp;height=71&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-10.png 350w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-10.png?width=700&amp;height=141&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-10.png 700w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-10.png?width=1050&amp;height=212&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-10.png 1050w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-10.png?width=1400&amp;height=282&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-10.png 1400w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-10.png?width=1750&amp;height=353&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-10.png 1750w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-10.png?width=2100&amp;height=423&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-10.png 2100w" style="height: auto; max-width: 100%; width: 700px; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block;" width="700"/> </p> <p> The list of services targeted by Beast Ransomware is as following: </p> <p> <span style="font-size: 14px;"> <img alt="beast-ransomware-blog-11" height="464" loading="lazy" sizes="(max-width: 700px) 100vw, 700px" src="https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-11.png?width=700&amp;height=464&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-11.png" srcset="https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-11.png?width=350&amp;height=232&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-11.png 350w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-11.png?width=700&amp;height=464&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-11.png 700w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-11.png?width=1050&amp;height=696&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-11.png 1050w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-11.png?width=1400&amp;height=928&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-11.png 1400w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-11.png?width=1750&amp;height=1160&amp;name=beast-ransomware-blog-11.png 1750w, https://www.cybereason.com/hs-fs/hubfs/dam/images/images-web/blog-images/beast-ransomware-blog-11.png?width=2100&amp;height=1392&amp;name=beast-rans