[31m==> The first enhanced one: [0m Incident: Beast Ransomware

Root cause: Initial compromises typically occur through phishing emails or compromised remote desktop protocol (RDP) endpoints.

Threat Actor/group/campaign: Beast Ransomware group

Organization/industry/location: Various industries, no specific organization or location mentioned.

Start date – End date: Active since 2022, with recent updates promoted in June 2024.

MITRE TTPs: 
- Execution: T1047 – Windows Management Instrumentation, T1106 - Native API
- Persistence: T1543.003 – Create or Modify System Process: Windows Service
- Discovery: T1083 - File and Directory Discovery, T1135 - Network Share Discovery, T1016 - System Network Configuration Discovery
- Privilege Escalation: T1078.001 – Valid Accounts: Default Accounts, T1078.002 – Valid Accounts: Domain Accounts
- Defense Evasion: T1406.002 – Obfuscated Files or Information: Software Packing, T1620 - Reflective Code Loading
- Lateral Movement: T1021.002 - Remote Service: SMB/Windows Admin Shares
- Collection: T1119 – Automated Collection
- Impact: T1486 - Data Encrypted for Impact, T1489 – Service Stop, T1490 – Inhibit System Recovery

Impact: Data encryption across Windows, Linux, and ESXi systems, significant data loss and operational disruption for affected organizations.

Mitigation:
- Follow and hunt Beast affiliate activity to identify pre-ransomware behaviors.
- Promote cybersecurity best practices such as multifactor authentication and patch management.
- For Cybereason customers: 
  - Enable Anti-Malware and set the Anti-Malware > Signatures mode to Prevent, Quarantine, or Disinfect.
  - Enable Anti-Ransomware (PRP), set Anti-Ransomware to Quarantine mode, and enable shadow copy protection.
  - Enable Application Control.
  - Keep systems fully patched to mitigate vulnerabilities.
  - Regularly back up files and create a backup process and policy.
  - Enable Variant Payload Prevention with prevent mode on Cybereason Behavioral execution prevention.

Detection Signature:
    Service: Windows Services, SMB/Windows Admin Shares
    Severity: Critical
    Incident: Beast Ransomware
    Signature name: “Suspicious Service Stoppages and Shadow Copy Deletions”
    Internal checks:
        - Monitor for the creation of mutex with the string “BEAST HERE?”
        - Watch for the use of RstrtMgr.dll to stop services and processes.
    External scanning:
        - Monitor for connections to iplogger.co for geofencing.
        - Look for the presence of the following SHA-256 hashes on systems.

