s.\n\nBeast stands out for its extensive tools and regular updates that appeal to a wide range of users within the cybercrime community. Attackers can design specialized payloads aimed at maximizing damage. For Windows, the ransomware employs advanced encryption techniques such as Elliptic-curve cryptography paired with ChaCha20, utilizing a multithreaded process for efficient file encryption while shutting down essential services to minimize data disruption.\n\nThe platform’s cross-operational capability is significant. The Windows variant can package files into ZIP archives containing ransom notes, while the Linux and ESXi versions are designed to halt virtual machines before executing encryption. A recent update introduced an offline builder, enabling the creation of ransomware specifically for Windows, NAS, and ESXi systems without needing an internet connection, thus increasing its adaptability.\n\nA particularly concerning aspect of Beast is its ability to self-propagate via SMB scans, which allows it to swiftly infect vulnerable systems within the same network. Interestingly, it avoids encrypting devices in Commonwealth of Independent States (CIS) countries, likely to evade local law enforcement. To obstruct recovery attempts, Beast also targets Windows shadow copies, eliminating backups to ensure victims cannot restore their files.\nFacebook\nTwitter\nPinterest\nWhatsApp\nLinkedIn\n×\nIf you are new user click here to\nsubscribe\nSUBMIT\nClose\n×\nDownload PDF\nBeast Ransomware RaaS Service Platform Targets Windows, Linux, and VMware ESXi\nA recent analysis by Cybereason highlights the evolving landscape of Beast Ransomware, a Ransomware-as-a-Service (RaaS) platform that has been actively targeting organizations since 2022. Known also as Monster, this malware has gained traction among cybercriminals for its adaptable features, allowing affiliates to customize attacks across various operating systems, including Windows, Linux, and VMware ESXi servers.\n\nBeas